      "id": 1,
      "text": "Paulina began as an Acadian exile settlement in 1765, when eleven Bourgeois families received land grants in 1803.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paulina_louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Paulina sits on the east bank of the Mississippi River, west of Lutcher and directly across from Vacherie.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paulina%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town is a historic center for perique tobacco, the rare fermented crop that earned St. James the nickname 'Perique Tobacco Capital.'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paulina_louisiana",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Paulina faces hurricane threats, with storm surges, high winds, and prolonged flooding caused by its flat topography and coastal influences.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paulina_louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Creole communities in Paulina built raised cottages to cope with the humid climate and flood‑prone terrain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paulina_louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Paulina lies at only 7 feet above sea level, surrounded by swampy wetlands and bayous that make it prone to erosion.",
